---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CategoryStrip from '../components/CategoryStrip.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import Doc from '../components/Doc.astro';
import AuthorCard from '../components/AuthorCard.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { categories } from '../components/data';
import { getArticles, getAuthors, ofType } from '../components/articles';
import { breadcrumbLd } from '../components/seo';

const authors = await getAuthors();
const articles = await getArticles();
const count = (id: string) => articles.filter((a) => a.data.author.id === id).length;
const reviews = ofType(articles, 'review').length;
---

<Layout
  title="About OSI Affiliate — who reviews the software, and how"
  description="OSI Affiliate is a small staff desk that reviews, scores and compares business software for solopreneurs and small teams. Who we are, what we cover, and how the site pays for itself."
  path="/about"
  jsonld={[breadcrumbLd([{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About', href: '/about' }])]}
>
  <CategoryStrip />
  <main id="main">
    <PageHead
      title="A small desk that reviews software for small businesses"
      lede="Choosing a CRM, an email tool or a website builder is a decision most owners make once, in a hurry, from a page written by a vendor. We wanted the page a knowledgeable friend would have written instead."
    />

    <Doc>
      <section>
        <h2>What the site is</h2>
        <p>
          OSI Affiliate covers {categories.length} categories of business software: {categories.map((c) => c.name).join(", ")}. In each one we
          review and score the tools a solopreneur or a team under twenty is actually choosing
          between, keep one short shortlist, and write the comparisons that decide between the
          picks. There are {reviews} full reviews on the site today, and every one has a staff name
          on it.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is written for the owner replacing a specific tool, not for a procurement team. So
          each review says who the tool is for, who should skip it, and what the bill looks like a
          year in, in plain words and without a login or a demo call.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How it started</h2>
        <p>
          The site began as a set of shortlists: two to four picks per category, with the reasoning
          shown and the affiliate model stated on the page instead of buried in it. That premise has
          not changed. What changed is who does the judging. Shortlists on their own are an opinion;
          a review is an opinion with the work shown. So the site now runs on staff reviews: a
          member of the desk signs up for the tool, tests it against the same five criteria as every
          other tool, and writes down what they found.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/how-we-pick">methodology page</a> is the full method, including its limits.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How we work</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Everyone on the desk has used the category of tool they review</b> in a business of
            their own or for a client, before reviewing it here.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>We test in our own accounts</b>, on the plan a reader would buy, and the review says
            when a vendor gave us a demo account instead.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Every review is read by a second member of staff</b> before it is published, and
            disagreements go in the review rather than being smoothed over.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>We say plainly which links pay us.</b> The disclosure is on every page with a vendor
            link, at body size, and a commission has never moved a score or a position.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Meet the desk</h2>
        <p>
          The people who write, test and check what is published here. A profile marked as not
          filled in yet is exactly that: a slot on the desk waiting for its person.
        </p>
        <div class="about-team">
          {authors.map((a) => <AuthorCard author={a} variant="row" articleCount={count(a.id)} as="h3" />)}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Get in touch</h2>
        <p>
          A tool we should review, a pricing line that has gone stale, a caveat you think is unfair,
          or a comparison you wanted and could not find: all of it is worth a message, and all of
          it is read by a person.
        </p>
        <p class="about-cta">
          <Button href="/contact" variant="solid">Contact the desk</Button>
        </p>
      </section>
    </Doc>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-team {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .about-cta {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
